<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve conflicts in group settings. Text: Summarize complex texts for peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can resolve simple conflicts in group study sessions by suggesting fair solutions and using clarifying language (e.g., says, "Let's split the tasks evenly so everyone has a fair part. I'll take notes, and you can make the slides."). Can summarize the main points of an academic article for peers, highlighting key advice and strategies (e.g., "This article explains that setting study goals improves focus. Juan pointed out that reviewing notes daily helps retain information better."). Can mediate disagreements in group projects by rephrasing ideas and ensuring everyone's opinions are heard (e.g., "Mariana suggested that if we combine both ideas, we can create a stronger project. What does everyone think?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher presents a short, hypothetical scenario: "Imagine you're in a study group, and two of your classmates, Alex and Sofia, disagree strongly on the best strategy to organize your 'Study Tips Digital Guide'. Alex thinks it should be organized by subject, and Sofia thinks it should be by learning style. How would you help them find a solution so everyone can achieve academic success?" Students share initial thoughts briefly in pairs. This activates prior knowledge on conflict resolution and academic goals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models phrases for mediation and summarizing by rephrasing one student's idea for another. For example, "So, if I understand correctly, Juan, you're suggesting that if we combine both ideas, we could have a section for different subjects, and within each, tips for various learning styles. Is that right?" The teacher models the pronunciation of /gr/ in "growth" and /kr/ in "create" while giving an example of a good strategy. Emphasizes inclusive and encouraging language: "Let's hear everyone's potential solutions."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'mediate' a disagreement?" (Expected answer: "To help people find a solution," or "To make sure everyone's ideas are heard.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to use clarifying language when mediating?" (Expected answer: "To ensure everyone understands each other," or "To avoid misunderstandings.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short academic article excerpt (approx. 150-200 words) titled "Effective Study Strategies for High School Success" (genre: informational article, register: formal, format: digital text). The article contains advice using modals (should, must, can) and implicitly sets up scenarios for the second conditional. Key vocabulary like "achievement," "focus," "motivation," "time management," and "discipline" are present.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Summarizing complex texts for peers and providing actionable advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main advice this article offers for academic improvement?" (Students read individually and identify the core message.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match key strategies from the article (e.g., "Set SMART goals," "Active recall") with their corresponding benefits mentioned in the text (e.g., "improves retention," "enhances focus").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points out examples of second conditional and modals for advice within the text, e.g., "If I were a student, I would try this strategy," or "You should always review your notes."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using second conditional for giving advice, modals for advice (should, must, can), and future simple for first conditional. Vocabulary: achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, feedback, progress, solution.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation & Summarization:</b> The teacher provides complex sentences or short paragraphs from the academic article. Students work individually to rephrase these into simpler, actionable advice for a ninth grader, using modals ("You should...", "You must...") or second conditional ("If I were you, I would...", "If you focused on this, you would..."). For example, "The article posits that consistent effort is a prerequisite for notable academic development." becomes "The article suggests you must put in consistent effort if you want to develop your academic skills." Teacher reminds students to be mindful of the stress patterns for words like "progress" (noun vs. verb) if they use it.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Mediation Dialogue Completion:</b> Students receive short dialogues with gaps representing conflict or misunderstanding in a study group. They complete the gaps using mediation phrases, clarifying questions, and target grammar/vocabulary to suggest solutions. E.g., "Student A: I think our digital guide should only focus on study skills. Student B: No, it also needs time management. Mediator: (gap: 'Perhaps we could include both ideas if we...'). If we ________ (combine / focus), we ________ (will create) a more comprehensive guide." The teacher encourages the use of phrases like "What study strategies do you recommend?" and "Let's share techniques that have worked for us."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Peer Advice & Clarification:</b> Students work in pairs. One student (Mediator) selects a key strategy from the academic article or from their own "Study Tips Digital Guide" project. The other student (Ninth Grader) pretends not to understand or presents a slight disagreement. The Mediator must summarize the strategy, clarify points, and mediate any simulated conflict using appropriate language. The roles then switch. Focus on clear pronunciation of contrastive sounds like /g/ and /k/ in "good" and "could" when giving advice.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Presentation and Mediation Role-Play. Students work in groups of 3-4. They assume the roles of "Guide Presenter," "Mediator/Clarifier," and "Ninth Graders."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Activity:</b> Each group presents their "Study Tips Digital Guide" (the 21st Century Skills Project) to the class. One student acts as the "Guide Presenter," explaining the key sections and advice. Another student acts as the "Mediator/Clarifier," whose role is crucial: if a "Ninth Grader" (another group member) asks a confusing question, expresses disagreement, or misunderstands a piece of advice, the Mediator steps in. The Mediator must rephrase the advice, summarize key strategies, suggest fair solutions if there's an internal group disagreement about how to present a tip, or use clarifying language to ensure the "Ninth Grader" fully grasps the concept. They must integrate modals for advice (e.g., "You should try using a planner to keep track of deadlines."), second conditional for hypothetical situations ("If you were more organized, you would achieve your goals."), and first conditional ("If you focus on your studies, you will achieve academic goals."). They also need to utilize sociolinguistic strategies like "Mariana suggested that if we combine both ideas, we can create a stronger project."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Focus on fluent communication, collaborative interaction, and effective use of mediation strategies to ensure clarity and resolve simulated minor conflicts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the group presentations, students acting as "Mediators" will be assessed on their ability to effectively mediate, summarize, and clarify information. Other students (as "Ninth Graders" and observers) will also offer peer feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Intelligibility:</b> Was the mediator's speech clear and easy to understand (including pronunciation of focus sounds /gr/, /kr/, /g/, /k/ and stress patterns)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Did the mediator effectively use vocabulary related to academic achievement and skill development (e.g., achievement, improvement, strategy, solution)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Application of Grammar (Modals/Conditionals):</b> Did the mediator correctly use modals for advice and second/first conditionals in their explanations and suggestions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation Effectiveness:</b> Did the mediator successfully summarize, rephrase ideas, suggest solutions, and ensure everyone's opinions were heard and understood?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Language:</b> Did the mediator use inclusive and encouraging language to facilitate discussion?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Rate your ability to mediate disagreements in your group today (1=needs practice, 5=excellent). Explain why."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new phrase or strategy you learned today for clarifying advice or resolving conflict?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-6 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing mediation skills today help you better understand the 'Study Tips Digital Guide' and academic advice in general?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies or language for mediation do you plan to use in future group projects or discussions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation-focused lesson? (e.g., students effectively used second conditional for advice, engaged in peer mediation, specific vocabulary was well-integrated).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to further develop their mediation skills in future lessons or projects? (e.g., more structured scenarios for conflict resolution, explicit modeling of rephrasing complex ideas, additional practice with contrastive sounds).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is..." theme. Students have now developed the content for their "Study Tips Digital Guide" and practiced mediating its advice. The next unit will build on these communication skills by moving to a new theme, possibly focusing on presenting their guides to a wider school audience or applying the learned academic strategies to a specific subject, requiring further integration of advice-giving and explanatory language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>